= Conjecture =

== Overview ==
A '''conjecture''' is a mathematical statement proposed as true but not yet proved or disproved in the relevant framework.

A conjecture can be motivated by examples, computation, analogy, patterns, heuristics, or partial results.

== Evidence and proof ==
Strong evidence can make a conjecture plausible.

However:

<pre>
many confirming cases ≠ proof
</pre>

A universal mathematical claim can fail because of a single counterexample.

A proof establishes the result generally; a counterexample refutes it.

== Conjecture and theorem ==
Once a conjecture is proved, it becomes a theorem or proved result.

If it is disproved, the original conjecture is false, although modified versions can remain useful.

== Conjecture and hypothesis ==
A mathematical conjecture is related to a scientific [[Hypothesis]] because both can be proposed before decisive justification.

But scientific hypotheses are usually evaluated through empirical evidence and testing, while mathematical conjectures are settled by proof or disproof relative to formal assumptions.

== Role in research ==
Conjectures organize mathematical investigation.

They can connect separate results, suggest new definitions, motivate methods, and expose gaps in existing theory even before they are resolved.

